  "final_text": "I built the dashboard and created the alert rule, and the rule is enabled. Its status is `ok`, which means its first run went through without errors. It hasn't fired yet, and I haven't checked that its query actually picks out 5xx requests.\n\n**1. What the data holds.** The data view `Kibana Sample Data Logs` uses `timestamp` as its time field. Each document is one web request, with these main fields:\n- **Response code:** `response`, a text field with a `response.keyword` copy for exact matching.\n- **Request details:** `request`, `url` and `extension`.\n- **Size:** `bytes`.\n- **Who sent it:** `clientip`, `agent` and `machine.os`.\n- **Where from:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest` and `geo.coordinates`.\n- **Other:** `host`, `referer` and `tags`.\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-dda01666 Web logs overview\"** has 7 panels: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-dda01666-web-logs-overview-f988437176ea\n- Three single numbers: total requests, unique visitors (distinct `clientip`) and total bytes served.\n- A bar chart of requests over time, split by response code.\n- A pie chart of how requests divide across response codes.\n- A table of the top 10 URLs, with request count and average bytes.\n- A bar chart of requests by operating system.\n\n**3. Alert rule \"EFF-dda01666 5xx spike\"** (id `2f75d388-805e-46bb-aca3-cffef3c55602`):\n- It's an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs` and runs every minute.\n- It counts requests whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" in the last 5 minutes, using the `timestamp` field.\n- It fires when that count is more than 10.\n- It has no notification connector, so when it fires the alert shows up in Kibana but nobody gets a message.",
